# Secret. Copy to ${SF_SECRETS_DIR}/backup.env (outside the repository, 0600). Read by: backup (pg_dump) and, for the
# LIVE side only, restore-check (the identity read that proves the restore target is not the live database).
# It is the READ-ONLY role force_backup, created by db-roles.sql (LOGIN, CONNECT on the database, membership in
# pg_read_all_data: SELECT on every table and sequence, nothing else; no write, no DDL, no CREATE, no TEMPORARY).
# It is NOT the owner role: migrate.env (force_migrator) is no longer read by backup, restore-check or the
# dependency of migrate-safe. Staging credentials only: never reuse production credentials (OPS-1).
#
# Same URL shape and rules as migrate.env.example (postgres://USER:PASSWORD@HOST[:PORT]/DBNAME, query limited to
# sslmode, sslrootcert, connect_timeout; percent-encode reserved characters in the password). The password is the
# FORCE_BACKUP_PASSWORD given to the owner's db-roles run (db-admin.env). Default initial staging (self-hosted-db):
#   postgres://force_backup:PASSWORD@postgres:5432/DB
DATABASE_URL=postgres://force_backup:REPLACE_PASSWORD@REPLACE_HOST:5432/REPLACE_DB?sslmode=verify-full
# With sslmode=verify-full against a managed database, add &sslrootcert=<mounted CA file path> and mount that
# file read-only in backup and restore-check (see migrate.env.example).
#
# Optional (compose env file, not this one): SF_OPS_USER=<uid>:<gid> is the user the ops containers run as; it must
# own (or be able to write) SF_BACKUP_DIR on the host. See README-staging.md "Backup and restore".
